/**
\defgroup distrib_pig Poisson-inverse Gaussian
\brief Discrete count distribution with mean \f$\mu > 0\f$ and shape \f$\alpha > 0\f$, diagonal expected information, and elementary log-mass evaluation.

Defined in `<laplace/distrib/pig.hpp>` as \ref laplace::distrib::pig_distrib "pig_distrib", this module evaluates the Poisson-inverse Gaussian log-mass, its parameter derivatives to order \f$6\f$, and its closed expected Fisher information blocks with derivatives to order \f$4\f$. Every evaluator is marked `LAPLACE_HD` so it runs on host CPUs and inside Kokkos device kernels (see \ref distrib_core "distrib_core"). Cumulative functions, derivatives of \f$\log F\f$ and \f$\log(1 - F)\f$, quantiles, and random numbers come from \ref distrib_generic "the generic routes". Both the value and the derivatives of orders \f$1\f$ to \f$4\f$ of the shape block of the expected information use the generic sweep when `scale()` is at most \f$4\f$ and a cut sum with a closed tail above \f$4\f$. Against high-precision reference values, the log-mass stays within \f$6.24\f$ ulp and derivatives of orders \f$2\f$ to \f$6\f$ stay within \f$201\f$ ulp, with one corner below the \f$\alpha = \mu\f$ split reaching \f$74.4\f$ ulp at order \f$1\f$.

## Classic form and parametrization

The Poisson-inverse Gaussian family arises as a Poisson distribution of mean \f$\mu Z\f$ mixed over an inverse Gaussian variable \f$Z\f$ of mean \f$1\f$ (\ref distrib_invgauss "inverse Gaussian"), giving a count distribution with a heavier tail than the negative binomial (\ref distrib_negbin2 "the quadratic negative binomial"). In the orthogonal parametrization of Heller, Couturier and Heritier (2019), written in terms of the mean \f$\mu > 0\f$, the shape \f$\alpha > 0\f$, and \f$s = \sqrt{\mu^2 + \alpha^2}\f$, the probability mass function is
\f[
P(Y = y) = \sqrt{\frac{2\alpha}{\pi}}\, e^{s - \mu} \left(\frac{\mu\alpha}{\mu + s}\right)^y \frac{K_{y - 1/2}(\alpha)}{y!}, \qquad y = 0, 1, 2, \dots,
\f]
where \f$K_\nu\f$ is the modified Bessel function of the second kind. The distribution has mean \f$\mu\f$ and variance \f$\mu + \mu^2 / (s - \mu)\f$. At fixed \f$\mu\f$, letting \f$\alpha \to \infty\f$ yields the Poisson distribution of mean \f$\mu\f$ (\ref distrib_poisson "distrib_poisson"), while \f$\alpha \to 0\f$ is the heavy-tailed end where the variance grows as \f$2\mu^3 / \alpha^2\f$.

## Parameters, domain, and regularity

Struct `pig_distrib` sets `kind = distrib_kind::discrete` and `n_params = 2`. Both `mu` and `alpha` have the open domain \f$(0, \infty)\f$ and report `infinitely_smooth` for both the log-mass and the cumulative functions, with no kink (`param_kinks()` returns an empty set) and a fixed support from \f$0\f$ to \f$+\infty\f$. Off the support, `logpmf()` returns \f$-\infty\f$. Member `center()` returns \f$\mu\f$, `scale()` returns the standard deviation \f$\sqrt{\mu + \mu^2/(s - \mu)}\f$, `radius()` returns \f$s\f$, and `excess()` returns \f$s - \mu = \alpha^2 / (s + \mu)\f$ so that the difference \f$s - \mu\f$ is never formed. Parameter derivatives are packed as `sym_tensor<2, K>`, whose component \f$b\f$ holds \f$\partial^K \ell / \partial\mu^{K - b} \partial\alpha^b\f$ (\ref distrib_core "distrib_core").

## Formulas and cancellation-free evaluation

Because the Bessel order \f$y - 1/2\f$ is always half an odd integer, \f$K_{y - 1/2}(\alpha)\f$ is elementary: with \f$m = \max(y - 1, 0)\f$,
\f[
K_{y - 1/2}(\alpha) = \sqrt{\frac{\pi}{2\alpha}}\, e^{-\alpha} \sum_{k=0}^{m} \frac{(m + k)!}{k!\,(m - k)!} (2\alpha)^{-k},
\f]
a sum of positive terms. Its \f$\sqrt{\alpha}\f$ factor cancels the mass's \f$\sqrt{\alpha}\f$ identically, so no Bessel routine (\ref bessel_k "bessel_k"), no uniform expansion, and no table is used. Setting \f$\theta = \log(2\alpha)\f$ and shifting the index to \f$j = y - k\f$ from \f$1\f$ to \f$y\f$ leaves one exponential family:
\f[
\ell = (s - \mu) - \alpha + y \log\mu - y \log(\mu + s) - y \log 2 + \log Q(\theta) - \log y!,
\f]
\f[
Q(\theta) = \sum_{j=1}^{y} a_j e^{j\theta}, \qquad a_j = \frac{(2y - 1 - j)!}{(y - j)!\,(j - 1)!},
\f]
with \f$Q = 1\f$ at \f$y = 0\f$, where the mass is \f$e^{s - \mu - \alpha}\f$ exactly. For \f$y \ge 1\f$, `logpmf()` walks the terms of \f$Q\f$ outwards from the largest term, whose index \f$j^*\f$ is the root in \f$[1, y]\f$ of \f$j^2 - (2y - 1 + 2\alpha)j + 2\alpha y = 0\f$ adjusted to the larger neighbour, using the exact ratio \f$2\alpha(y - j) / (j(2y - 1 - j))\f$ and stopping when a term falls below \f$2^{-80}\f$ of the largest; the cost therefore tracks the width of the weights rather than \f$y\f$. To avoid \f$y \log y\f$ cancellation at large counts and small \f$\alpha\f$, the log-gammas at \f$j^*\f$ and \f$y!\f$ are grouped through Stirling's error (\ref stirling "stirling"), \f$\operatorname{log1p}\f$, and \f$s - \mu = \alpha^2/(s + \mu)\f$, while \f$s - \mu - \alpha\f$ is formed as \f$\mu^2/(s + \alpha) - \mu\f$ when \f$\alpha \ge \mu\f$ and \f$\alpha^2/(s + \mu) - \alpha\f$ otherwise.

Pure derivatives in \f$\alpha\f$ use the fact that \f$\mathrm{d}^q \log Q / \mathrm{d}\theta^q\f$ is the \f$q\f$-th cumulant of \f$j\f$ under the weights \f$a_j e^{j\theta}/Q\f$, obtained from the central moments of the same outward walk and composed with \f$\theta = \log(2\alpha)\f$ via \ref faa_di_bruno "faa_di_bruno". The first cumulant is the mean of \f$j\f$, which is at least \f$1\f$, so nothing cancels inside that block. The remaining pure \f$\alpha\f$ terms come from \f$s\f$ and \f$\log(\mu + s)\f$, using
\f[
\frac{\mathrm{d}^q s}{\mathrm{d}\alpha^q} = \mu^2 \frac{\mathrm{d}^{q-2}}{\mathrm{d}\alpha^{q-2}} (\mu^2 + \alpha^2)^{-3/2}
\f]
for \f$q \ge 2\f$ (avoiding the cancellation in \f$1/s - \alpha^2/s^3\f$ when \f$\alpha \gg \mu\f$) and \f$-\mu^2 / (s(s + \alpha))\f$ for \f$\alpha/s - 1\f$ at \f$q = 1\f$.

At the first order, \f$\kappa_1/\alpha\f$ and \f$y\alpha / (s(\mu + s))\f$ both tend to \f$y/\alpha\f$ as \f$\alpha\f$ grows, and cancel. Because \f$\alpha^2 = s^2 - \mu^2\f$ and \f$s(\mu + s) - \alpha^2 = \mu(s + \mu)\f$, the second term is exactly \f$(y/\alpha)(1 - \mu/s)\f$, so where \f$\alpha \ge \mu\f$ the order is evaluated as

\f[
  \frac{\partial \log p}{\partial \alpha} = -\frac{\mu^2}{s(s + \alpha)} + \frac{\kappa_1 - y}{\alpha} + \frac{y\mu}{\alpha s},
\f]

with \f$\kappa_1 - y\f$ formed as \f$(j^* - y) + m_1/W\f$, where \f$m_1\f$ is the first moment of the walk about \f$j^*\f$ and \f$W\f$ its scaled sum, both of them exact. Below the split that form would divide \f$\kappa_1 - y\f$, which is of the size of \f$y\f$ there, by a small \f$\alpha\f$, so the first form is kept there instead.

Evaluating the tail integral of the shape block of the expected information treats \f$y\f$ as a real number, which the half-integer polynomial \f$Q(\theta)\f$ cannot accept because its sum runs over integer \f$y\f$. At a real count \f$y\f$, the mass and its derivative in \f$\alpha\f$ therefore call \ref laplace::math::own::log_bessel_k "log_bessel_k()" (whose uniform branch in \ref bessel_k "bessel_k" covers the large orders) and its derivative in the argument \ref laplace::math::own::log_bessel_k_dx1 "log_bessel_k_dx1()":
\f[
\log p(y) = \frac{1}{2}\log\left(\frac{2\alpha}{\pi}\right) + (s - \mu) + y \log\left(\frac{\mu\alpha}{\mu + s}\right) + \log K_{y - 1/2}(\alpha) - \log\Gamma(y + 1),
\f]
\f[
\frac{\partial \log p}{\partial \alpha} = \frac{1}{2\alpha} + \frac{\alpha}{s} + y\left(\frac{1}{\alpha} - \lambda'\right) + \frac{\mathrm{d}\log K_{y - 1/2}(\alpha)}{\mathrm{d}\alpha},
\f]
with \f$\lambda' = \alpha / (s(\mu + s))\f$. Against `logpmf()` and `logpmf_d1()` at integer counts up to \f$3000\f$, these two agree to \f$3.1 \times 10^{-13}\f$ and \f$3.7 \times 10^{-13}\f$ of the value.

Every partial of \f$\log p\f$ in \f$(\mu, \alpha)\f$ is also available at a non-integer count \f$y\f$, because \f$\mu\f$ does not enter the Bessel order. Components with at least one derivative in \f$\mu\f$ use the Leibniz form of the score in \f$\mu\f$ given below, an identity in \f$y\f$ whose constant \f$-1\f$ belongs only to that first-order component since every further derivative eliminates it. For order \f$q \ge 1\f$, the pure derivatives in \f$\alpha\f$ come from the real-count mass as
\f[
\ell_{0,q} = \frac{1}{2}\frac{(-1)^{q-1}(q-1)!}{\alpha^q} + s^{(q)} + y\left(\frac{(-1)^{q-1}(q-1)!}{\alpha^q} - (\log(\mu + s))^{(q)}\right) + \frac{\mathrm{d}^q \log K_{y - 1/2}(\alpha)}{\mathrm{d}\alpha^q},
\f]
where \f$\ell_{0,q} = \partial^q \log p / \partial\alpha^q\f$, superscript \f$(q)\f$ denotes the \f$q\f$-th derivative in \f$\alpha\f$, and \ref bessel_k "bessel_k" supplies the derivatives of \f$\log K_{y - 1/2}(\alpha)\f$ to order \f$7\f$ via \ref laplace::math::own::log_bessel_k_dx_upto "log_bessel_k_dx_upto()". Against `logpmf_dk()` at integer counts to order \f$5\f$ on the `gcc` build, the real-count partials agree to \f$1.9 \times 10^{-12}\f$ of the scale of the order over \f$\mu \in [0.5, 100]\f$, \f$\alpha \in [10^{-3}, 10]\f$, and counts up to \f$3000\f$.

In the mean, the terms of the size of \f$y\f$ cancel identically, leaving the exact score
\f[
\frac{\partial \ell}{\partial \mu} = c\,(y - \mu), \qquad c = \frac{1}{\mu} - \frac{1}{s}.
\f]
The first order takes that product as it stands, which is exactly \f$0\f$ at \f$y = \mu\f$. Every higher derivative, with \f$p \ge 1\f$ differentiations in \f$\mu\f$ and \f$q\f$ in \f$\alpha\f$, takes it expanded,
\f[
\frac{\partial^{p+q}\ell}{\partial\mu^p \partial\alpha^q} = y\, c_{p-1,q} + m_{p-1,q}, \qquad m = \frac{\mu}{s},
\f]
which is algebraically the same as \f$(y - \mu) c_{p-1,q} - (p - 1) c_{p-2,q}\f$, since \f$\mu c_{p-1} + (p-1) c_{p-2} = -m_{p-1}\f$, but does not split a result of the size of \f$\alpha^{-(p-1)}\f$ into terms of the size of one at the Poisson limit. Here \f$c_{i,j} = \partial^{i+j} c / \partial\mu^i \partial\alpha^j\f$ and \f$m_{i,j}\f$ likewise. Both \f$c\f$ and \f$m\f$ are functions of the one ratio \f$\alpha/\mu\f$, and which of its two powers is the small one decides the composition, because an inner map that is large makes every term of the chain large while the result is not: with \f$h(x) = 1 - (1 + x)^{-1/2}\f$, written \f$x / (w(w+1))\f$ with \f$w = \sqrt{1 + x}\f$ so that \f$1/\mu - 1/s\f$ never cancels, and \f$g(x) = (1 + x)^{-1/2} = 1 - h(x)\f$, the split is at \f$\alpha = \mu\f$,
\f[
\alpha \le \mu: \quad c = \frac{h(u)}{\mu}, \quad m = 1 - h(u), \quad u = \left(\frac{\alpha}{\mu}\right)^2; \qquad
\alpha > \mu: \quad c = \frac{1}{\mu} - \frac{g(v)}{\alpha}, \quad m = \frac{\mu}{\alpha} g(v), \quad v = \left(\frac{\mu}{\alpha}\right)^2.
\f]
In each branch the inner maps are products of a power of one parameter and a power of the other, so their partials are closed, and \ref chain_pair "chain_pair" composes them once per order.

The expected Fisher information is diagonal: \f$I_{\mu\mu} = 1/\mu - 1/s = c\f$ and \f$I_{\mu\alpha} = 0\f$ are exact, and the derivatives of \f$I_{\mu\mu}\f$ are the partials \f$c_{i,j}\f$ above. For the shape block, \f$I_{\alpha\alpha} = \sum_{y=0}^\infty (\partial\log p / \partial\alpha)^2 p(y)\f$: half of the sum sits at \f$y \le 1\f$ and \f$98\f$ per cent at \f$y \le 1000\f$, after which the terms fall as a power of \f$y\f$ until the geometric factor \f$e^{-\lambda y}\f$, with \f$\lambda = \operatorname{log1p}(\alpha^2 / (2\mu(s + \mu)))\f$, takes over at counts of the size of \f$\mu^2 / \alpha^2\f$. The generic sweep of \ref distrib_generic "distrib_generic" stops a direction when two neighbouring terms fall below \f$2^{-60}\f$ of the running sum of absolute values, so it needs counts of that order: \f$444\f$ terms at \f$\alpha = \mu\f$, \f$1.74 \times 10^4\f$ at \f$\alpha/\mu = 0.1\f$, \f$1.6 \times 10^6\f$ at \f$0.01\f$, and \f$1.7 \times 10^7\f$ at \f$0.003\f$, taking \f$0.4\f$ ms, \f$6\f$ ms, \f$0.5\f$ s, and \f$6\f$ s a call. At \f$\alpha/\mu = 10^{-3}\f$ and below it passes its cap of \f$2^{26}\f$ terms a direction and returns quiet NaN, while at \f$(\mu, \alpha) = (40, 10^{-2})\f$ it stops early on an underflowed mass and reports convergence \f$4.2 \times 10^{-5}\f$ away from the value. 

The cut route instead sums the first \f$1025\f$ counts (\f$y = 0, \dots, 1024\f$) term by term with `logpmf()` and `logpmf_d1()`, and adds the counts above \f$1024\f$ in closed form using the real-count formulas above. Because the real-count integrand \f$f(y) = (\partial\log p / \partial\alpha)^2 p(y)\f$ is smooth, the sum over integers above \f$1024\f$ is evaluated as \f$\int_{1024.5}^\infty f(y)\,\mathrm{d}y + f'(1024.5)/24\f$, adding the midpoint rule's first Euler-Maclaurin correction. The integral is taken by \ref laplace::math::quad::exp_sinh_quad "exp_sinh_quad()" with decay length \f$1/\lambda\f$, whose own error estimate never exceeds \f$5 \times 10^{-21}\f$ of the result. The derivative \f$f'(1024.5)\f$ comes from the tail model \f$A y^{-3/2} e^{-\lambda y} (a - \lambda' y)^2\f$, where \f$A\f$ is the \f$y\f$-independent prefactor that cancels in \f$f'/f\f$ and \f$a = 1/\alpha + \alpha/s\f$; that approximation suffices for a term that is \f$10^{-5}\f$ of the tail, moving the result from \f$5.3 \times 10^{-10}\f$ to \f$1.6 \times 10^{-11}\f$ of the value against the same sum cut at \f$16384\f$. 

Member `expected_info()` switches on `scale()`, calling the cut route above \f$4\f$ and the generic sweep at or below \f$4\f$. The cut route costs \f$0.42\f$ ms regardless of the parameters, whereas the sweep costs \f$0.09\f$ ms at a spread of \f$1.8\f$, \f$0.27\f$ ms at \f$3.1\f$, \f$0.70\f$ ms at \f$4.9\f$, and \f$6.0\f$ ms at \f$14\f$. Between spreads of \f$4\f$ and about \f$6\f$ the sweep can still be the faster of the two, by up to a factor of two at \f$(\mu, \alpha) = (3.5, 2)\f$ (\f$0.25\f$ ms against \f$0.43\f$ ms), while at \f$(1, 0.3)\f$, of spread \f$4.87\f$, it already costs \f$0.70\f$ ms against \f$0.41\f$ ms; a single threshold is kept because above it only the cut route stays right.

Member `expected_info_dk()` switches on `scale()` at the same threshold of \f$4\f$ for the derivatives of \f$I_{\alpha\alpha}\f$ of orders \f$1\f$ to \f$4\f$. Above \f$4\f$, it evaluates the score-form integrands of \ref distrib_generic "distrib_generic" using the family's integer-count partials for the first \f$1025\f$ counts and the real-count partials above \f$1024\f$, integrating the tail from \f$1024.5\f$. Because these derivatives carry powers of \f$y\f$, their tails weigh far more than the value's, from \f$1.6\f$ per cent to ninety times the component itself, and the integral is taken without the Euler-Maclaurin correction that the value uses. On the `gcc` build, the order-\f$4\f$ tensor costs \f$7.3\f$ ms at \f$(\mu, \alpha) = (10, 0.1)\f$ against the sweep's \f$11.0\f$ s, and \f$12.6\f$ ms at \f$(1, 10^{-3})\f$, where the sweep gives up after \f$310\f$ s. Between spreads of \f$4\f$ and about \f$6\f$ the cut route can be the slower of the two, costing \f$6.7\f$ ms against \f$4.2\f$ ms at \f$(3.5, 2)\f$.

The cumulative function, derivatives of \f$\log F\f$ and \f$\log(1 - F)\f$, quantiles, and random numbers are supplied by \ref distrib_generic "distrib_generic". Member `start_value()` inverts the moments, taking \f$\mu\f$ from the sample mean and \f$\alpha = \sqrt{d(d + 2\mu)}\f$ with \f$d = \mu^2 / (s^2_y - \mu)\f$, whose denominator is floored at \f$\mu / 100\f$ so that a sample with no overdispersion starts near the Poisson limit instead of at an end of the domain (\ref distrib_start "Starting values").

## Accuracy and limits

Against the \f$1532\f$ reference rows that `tools/reference/distrib_pig.py` computes with mpmath (The mpmath development team, 2023) from forms it first checks with SymPy (Meurer and others, 2017) -- the log-mass and its derivatives of orders \f$1\f$ to \f$6\f$ over \f$\mu \in [10^{-3}, 10^4]\f$, \f$\alpha \in [10^{-3}, 10^3]\f$ and counts up to \f$3000\f$, evaluated inside Kokkos kernels on the `gcc` and `CUDA` builds -- the errors stay within the test bounds below, and the two builds agree to three digits on every worst case. They are measured in ulps of the largest component of the same order at the same point, since every tensor of a given order has genuine zeros in \f$y\f$ and in the parameters (see \ref accuracy "How accuracy is measured").

| Order | Worst measured (ulp) | Test bound (ulp) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| \f$0\f$ (`logpmf`) | \f$6.24\f$ | \f$10\f$ |
| \f$1\f$ (`logpmf_d1`) | \f$74.4\f$ | \f$112\f$ |
| \f$2\f$ (`logpmf_d2`) | \f$5.81\f$ | \f$10\f$ |
| \f$3\f$ (`logpmf_d3`) | \f$10.4\f$ | \f$16\f$ |
| \f$4\f$ (`logpmf_d4`) | \f$19.7\f$ | \f$30\f$ |
| \f$5\f$ (`logpmf_d5`) | \f$99.6\f$ | \f$150\f$ |
| \f$6\f$ (`logpmf_d6`) | \f$201\f$ | \f$310\f$ |

Three limits apply. First, the order-\f$1\f$ peak of \f$74.4\f$ ulp sits at \f$(\mu, \alpha) = (100, 5)\f$ and \f$y = 949\f$, below the split, where the cumulant and the term of the count are summed as they stand; above the split the rearrangement above leaves \f$2.6\f$ ulp. Second, on the `gcc` build, the cut route for the derivatives of \f$I_{\alpha\alpha}\f$ differs from the same route cut at \f$16384\f$ instead of \f$1024\f$ by at most \f$2.5 \times 10^{-8}\f$ of the scale of the order, at order \f$4\f$ and \f$(\mu, \alpha) = (1, 0.1)\f$, and by \f$5 \times 10^{-10}\f$ on most components, which is the size of the omitted Euler-Maclaurin term. Against the generic sweep where the sweep converges, the cut route agrees to \f$1.9 \times 10^{-15}\f$ of the scale at \f$(\mu, \alpha) = (3.5, 2)\f$ (spread \f$5.2\f$) and \f$2.5 \times 10^{-8}\f$ at \f$(1, 0.1)\f$, where the sweep sums \f$1.6 \times 10^5\f$ terms; at the Poisson limit the sweep converges in \f$26\f$ terms. In `tests/distrib/test_pig.cpp`, those comparisons against the sweep use tolerances of \f$10^{-13}\f$ of the scale at \f$(3.5, 2)\f$ and \f$10^{-7}\f$ at \f$(1, 0.1)\f$, and at the heavy-tailed end the test checks that every component is finite and that the derivatives agree with 5-point differences of the order below on the same route (tolerance \f$2 \times 10^{-4}\f$). For \f$I_{\alpha\alpha}\f$ itself, `tests/data/reference/pig_info.csv` holds \f$18\f$ rows written by `tools/reference/distrib_pig.py --info` across spreads from \f$0.58\f$ to \f$4.5 \times 10^4\f$ (reaching \f$\alpha/\mu = 10^{-4}\f$, four on the sweep and fourteen on the cut). The script sums the definition with mpmath at \f$40\f$ digits using its real-order Bessel function via \f$\mathrm{d}\log K_\nu/\mathrm{d}\alpha = -(K_{\nu-1} + K_{\nu+1})/(2K_\nu)\f$ and its own Euler-Maclaurin acceleration, so neither the half-integer polynomial nor the library's quadrature enters. On both the `gcc` and `CUDA` builds, `expected_info()` stays within \f$1.36 \times 10^{-11}\f$ of the reference at \f$(\mu, \alpha) = (10, 10^{-3})\f$, with every other row below \f$4.5 \times 10^{-12}\f$ and the four sweep rows within \f$1.9 \times 10^{-15}\f$ (test bound \f$2.1 \times 10^{-11}\f$). The Poisson limit is left out of the reference because mpmath's Bessel function is slow where order and argument are close and does not finish at \f$\alpha = 10^3\f$. Third, the cumulative functions have no reference table; `tests/distrib/test_pig.cpp` checks `logpmf()` against the direct assembly with \ref bessel_k "log_bessel_k()" for \f$y < 30\f$ (tolerance \f$10^{-11}\f$), `expected_info()` against the score variance (\f$10^{-6}\f$), the four generic cumulative quantities against an independent compensated summation of the mass over the whole support at five \f$(\mu, \alpha)\f$ (\f$8 \times 10^{-9}\f$, the worst measured being \f$2.03 \times 10^{-9}\f$ on the `CUDA` build far out in the tail of \f$(10, 0.5)\f$ and \f$2.8 \times 10^{-10}\f$ elsewhere), and generic cumulative derivatives up to order \f$3\f$ against 5-point finite differences (\f$10^{-5}\f$). See `docs/numerics.md` for full measurements.

## Kokkos usage

```cpp
#include <Kokkos_Core.hpp>
#include <laplace/distrib/pig.hpp>

void eval_pig(Kokkos::View<const double*> y, Kokkos::View<double*> out, double mu, double alpha) {
  const laplace::distrib::pig_distrib d{mu, alpha};
  Kokkos::parallel_for("pig_eval", y.extent(0), KOKKOS_LAMBDA(const int i) {
    out(i) = d.logpmf(y(i)) + d.logpmf_d1(y(i))[0];
  });
}
```

## Members

Struct \ref laplace::distrib::pig_distrib "pig_distrib" implements \ref distrib_core "distrib_core" for `n_params = 2`: `mu`, `alpha`, `radius()`, `excess()`, `support_lower()`, `support_upper()`, `center()`, `scale()`, `param_kinks()`, `logpmf()`, `logpmf_d1()`..`logpmf_d6()`, `logpmf_dk()`, `expected_info()`, `expected_info_d1()`..`expected_info_d4()`, `expected_info_dk()`, and `start_value()`.

## References

- G. Z. Heller, D.-L. Couturier and S. R. Heritier (2019), Beyond mean modelling: Bias due to misspecification of dispersion in Poisson-inverse Gaussian regression, Biometrical Journal, 61(2), 333–342. https://doi.org/10.1002/bimj.201700218
- A. Meurer and others (2017), SymPy: symbolic computing in Python, PeerJ Computer Science, 3, e103. https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj-cs.103
- The mpmath development team (2023), mpmath: a Python library for arbitrary-precision floating-point arithmetic, version 1.3.0, https://mpmath.org
*/
